

    - two things suffice to ground an ethic: the phenomenon of the cosmic safehold
      and the concept of moral law

               cosmic safehold  + ‘moral law’ →  ethic

        : re `cosmic safehold` see ~/work/safehold/ : *The presence throughout the cosmos
          of a safehold fit for securing our long-term survival.*
        : re `moral law` see `^*Pearsall.+2003.+Oxford dictionary of English`s @ sources.brec :
         ‘(in some systems of ethics) an absolute principle defining the criteria of right action
          (whether conceived as a divine ordinance or a truth of reason)’
        : re `ethic` see doi:10.1093/OED/2656971922 : *Oxford English dictionary*, ‘ethic (n.),
          sense b,’ June 2026. : ‘A system or set of moral principles’.
        - the first evidences a source of morals, the second represents its known conduit into society,
          and the conjunction of the two specifies a particular moral that together they convey
            : re `the first (.+ a source of morals)` see `^*- the cosmic safehold ${same}$`
        repair+ they cannot themselves ground, let alone suffice to, due to being contingent
            | rather they conduct [us|one] {to the grounds}|{in the grounding}, and suffice to
            | rather they pinpoint|locate the grounds, and suffice to
    - the cosmic safehold evidences a source of morals
        - because what explains the presence of that safehold?
        - a survey of possible explanantia yields (among others) only two of moral bearing:

            • supernatural design by God
                : re `^*• (.+ design) .+` see `^^${same}$` @ ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
            • mundane design by cosmic creators
                : re `^*• (.+ design) .+` see `^^${same}$` @ ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
                !! what is qualified cannot ground the absolute principle that is moral law
                    : re `absolute principle that is moral law` see
                      `^*: re .moral law.+an absolute principle`s
                    - the creators, in being contingent, might be taken as qualified in a sense
                      that makes them unfit to ground moral law
                    | refute the premise: the mundane cosmic creators are not so qualified
                        ∵ neither is the practical ground of their creative act, whether default or not,
                          so qualified
                            ∵ the default practical ground merely *contains* a qualification
                              as opposed to being *governed* by one
                                - it applies (its qualified content) unconditionally and universally
                    | elevate the qualification to the law
                        - let the qualified instead ground a qualified|contingent|default moral law
                      \ | let the scope of the moral law be cosmic, not universal
                      \     / thus a cosmic moral law, the moral law of our cosmos, so qualified
                      \     ∵ within the context of their creation, even mundane creators are necessary
                      \\    !! then cosmic creation would be amoral, out of moral scope
        ?+ better warrant (more encouraging) to set aside the others, is there?
